                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Judaism: Ha-Satan — The Accuser, Not the Enemy", body: "This might surprise you: in Judaism, Satan is not the cosmic enemy of God. He's an employee.\n\nHa-Satan in Hebrew means \"the adversary\" or \"the accuser.\" It started as a VERB (to oppose) and evolved into a title — not a name. In the Hebrew Bible (Tanakh), ha-Satan appears sparingly.\n\nIn Job 1:6-12, ha-Satan shows up in the heavenly court, among the \"sons of God,\" and challenges Job's piety. But he doesn't act independently — he asks God for permission, and God grants it. He's a prosecutor in God's court, not a rebel waging war.\n\nIn Zechariah 3:1-2, ha-Satan accuses the high priest Joshua, but God rebukes him. Again — subordinate, not autonomous.\n\nJudaism's strict monotheism means there is no dualistic war between equal powers. God is sovereign. Evil stems from human free will, not from a cosmic rival. The rabbinic concept of yetzer hara (evil inclination) places the source of temptation WITHIN the human, not outside.\n\nLater developments (Talmud, Midrash, Kabbalah) personify Satan more, sometimes linking him to the Angel of Death or the evil inclination. But even then, he's part of God's creation serving a purpose — TESTING, not rebelling.\n\nCritical nuance: Genesis 3's serpent is NOT identified as Satan in Jewish texts. That connection is a Christian interpretation. In Judaism, it's a clever animal. Full stop.\n\nThis matters because it reveals a fundamentally different architecture: Judaism says the darkness is INSIDE you (your choices, your inclination). Christianity says the darkness is also OUTSIDE you (a fallen entity actively opposing God)."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Christianity & Islam: Fallen Angel vs. Fallen Jinn", body: "CHRISTIANITY: THE COSMIC REBEL\nChristian theology develops Satan far beyond the Hebrew Bible. He becomes:\n• A fallen angel who rebelled against God (drawn from Isaiah 14:12-15, Ezekiel 28:12-17 — originally about earthly kings, later reinterpreted)\n• The serpent in Eden (Revelation 12:9 makes this explicit)\n• \"The god of this age\" who blinds minds (2 Corinthians 4:4)\n• A being defeated by Christ's death and resurrection but still active until final judgment\n\nChristianity's Satan is an autonomous enemy with his own will, his own followers (demons), and his own strategy. He's not testing you for God — he's actively trying to destroy you.\n\nThe New Testament dramatically expands the role: tempting Jesus (Matthew 4), possessing people (Mark 5), operating through human systems (Ephesians 6:12 — \"principalities and powers\"), and ultimately being defeated and cast into the lake of fire (Revelation 20:10).\n\nISLAM: IBLIS — THE JINN WHO REFUSED\nIn Islam, the adversary is Iblis (from Greek diabolos) or Shaytan (from Hebrew satan). But here's the key difference: Iblis is NOT a fallen angel. He's a JINN — a being made of smokeless fire.\n\nQuran 2:34 — God commands all beings to bow to Adam. The angels obey. Iblis refuses, saying: \"I am better than him. You created me from fire and him from clay.\"\n\nThat's the sin: PRIDE. The refusal to submit. The insistence on being superior.\n\nGod gives Iblis respite until Judgment Day, and Iblis vows to mislead humanity from every direction (Quran 7:16-17). But — and this is crucial — Iblis has NO power over those who are truly submitted to God. He can only whisper suggestions. He can't compel.\n\nQuran 14:22 — On Judgment Day, Iblis says to his followers: \"I had no authority over you except that I called you and you responded to me. So do not blame me; blame yourselves.\"\n\nThat's devastating. The adversary himself says: I only suggested. YOU chose.\n\nPATTERN: Judaism says evil is your own inclination. Christianity says evil is an external enemy AND your own weakness. Islam says evil is an external whisperer, but the choice is always yours."),
                ],
