                emoji: "🕉️",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Zoroastrianism: The Original Cosmic War", body: "Zoroastrianism may have influenced ALL other concepts of Satan. Founded by Zarathustra (likely 1500-500 BCE) in ancient Persia, it's the clearest example of cosmic dualism in any major religion.\n\nTwo primal spirits:\n• Ahura Mazda — the Wise Lord, source of truth, order, and light\n• Angra Mainyu (Ahriman) — the destructive spirit, source of lies, chaos, and darkness\n\nThese two have existed from the beginning, each choosing their nature. They're in ACTIVE cosmic war — not a test, not a court drama, but genuine battle. Every human must choose sides. Every thought, word, and deed either strengthens truth or strengthens the lie.\n\nZoroastrianism teaches that Ahura Mazda will ultimately triumph, and evil will be destroyed. History has a direction — from mixture (good and evil blended) toward separation (good purified, evil eliminated).\n\nWhy this matters: The Jewish exile in Babylon (6th century BCE) brought Israelites into direct contact with Zoroastrian ideas. Many scholars argue that post-exilic Judaism absorbed dualistic concepts — an increasingly personified Satan, angels and demons, final judgment, heaven and hell — that were less prominent in earlier Hebrew texts.\n\nIf that's true, then Christianity's cosmic Satan may be a Zoroastrian import, filtered through Second Temple Judaism, codified in the New Testament, and presented as original biblical theology.\n\nThis doesn't make it \"wrong\" — but it shows how ideas cross-pollinate across cultures. The same way we saw with energy concepts (Chi, Prana, Kundalini crossing borders), concepts of evil also travel and transform."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Buddhism & Hinduism: Mara & the Asuras", body: "BUDDHISM: MARA — THE TEMPTER OF ILLUSION\nBuddhism has no Satan because Buddhism has no creator God to rebel against. But it has Mara — the personification of everything that prevents awakening.\n\nMara tempted Siddhartha Gautama on the night of his enlightenment, sending armies of demons, beautiful daughters (desire), and psychological terror. The Buddha's response: he touched the earth and said \"The earth is my witness.\" Mara dissolved.\n\nMara represents: craving, aversion, ignorance — the three poisons that keep you trapped in samsara (the cycle of suffering). He's not an external entity waging war. He's the INTERNAL mechanism of delusion itself.\n\nWhen a Buddhist says \"Mara attacked me,\" they might mean: \"My ego tried to pull me back into unconscious patterns.\" The enemy isn't out there. The enemy IS the pattern of unconsciousness.\n\nThis maps directly to shadow work: Mara IS your shadow. The parts of you that resist awakening, that prefer the comfort of sleep, that whisper \"go back to autopilot.\"\n\nHINDUISM: ASURAS & THE COSMIC PLAY\nHinduism has asuras — powerful beings who oppose the devas (gods). But the relationship is more complex than \"good vs. evil.\" Asuras are often described as the elder brothers of the devas — same divine origin, different choices.\n\nIn some texts, asuras represent ego, attachment, and darkness. In others, they're necessary parts of cosmic balance. The universe needs both forces to function — creation and destruction, light and shadow.\n\nHinduism also has Ravana (the demon king in the Ramayana) who kidnaps Sita and is defeated by Rama — a clear good-vs-evil narrative. But even Ravana is portrayed as learned, powerful, and originally devoted. His fall comes from pride — same as Iblis.\n\nThe broadest Hindu view: all of existence is lila — divine play. Good and evil are both expressions of Brahman (ultimate reality). The \"enemy\" is maya (illusion) — not a being, but a veil over consciousness.\n\nYAZIDISM: THE MISUNDERSTOOD FAITH\nYazidism has been called \"devil worship\" by outsiders — a devastating mischaracterization. Yazidis revere Melek Taus (the Peacock Angel), who they say wept for 7,000 years in repentance, extinguishing the fires of hell with his tears. He's a figure of REDEMPTION, not evil.\n\nYazidis reject the concept of a supreme evil being. They believe God is so powerful that evil cannot exist as an independent force. Melek Taus is God's chief angel — a reconciled being, not a rebel.\n\nPATTERN ACROSS ALL:\n• Judaism: Evil = human choice (internal)\n• Christianity: Evil = fallen entity + human weakness (external + internal)\n• Islam: Evil = whisperer + human choice (external suggestion, internal decision)\n• Zoroastrianism: Evil = cosmic principle in active war (fully external, fully dualistic)\n• Buddhism: Evil = ignorance and craving (fully internal, no external entity)\n• Hinduism: Evil = illusion/ego within divine play (transcended, not defeated)\n• Yazidism: Evil = redeemed, not permanent\n\nEvery tradition maps the same territory differently. Some say the dragon is outside the cave. Some say the dragon IS the cave. Some say there is no dragon — only the fear of dragons."),
